<h2>A twelve-year chronology</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2014.</strong> The wife files a petition under Section 125 CrPC for herself and the minor daughter. An ex parte order of Rs 5,000 per month each follows in 2015.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2016&ndash;2017.</strong> The High Court remits the matter with interim maintenance of Rs 10,000 per month. On remand, the Family Court awards the daughter Rs 20,000 per month from January 2016, plus educational and unusual medical expenses; the wife&rsquo;s own claim is dismissed.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2018.</strong> In cross-revisions, a compromise is recorded: the father is to clear the mortgage on a flat and settle it on the daughter, with maintenance running from the 2014 petition date. He does not execute the settlement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2019&ndash;2022.</strong> The mother applies for the accumulated balance — about Rs 13.6 lakh net of payments. The Family Court, item by item, fixes Rs 6,14,887.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>28 October 2026.</strong> The High Court dismisses the father&rsquo;s revision and confirms the order.</div>
</div>

<h2>How the Family Court&rsquo;s arithmetic survived</h2>
<p>The revision attacked the quantification as mechanical. The High Court found the opposite: every established payment — including a lump sum of Rs 5,00,000 in 2021 — had been credited; school-fee payments the father proved were deducted; and heads outside the 2017 order, such as hobby classes and day-care, were excluded altogether. A general denial of bills, or the bare assertion that insurance <em>could</em> have been claimed, &ldquo;does not by itself establish that the child&rsquo;s actual and eligible expenses were not incurred.&rdquo; If the father paid the school twice, the court observed, his remedy lies against the school.</p>

<h2>The principles the order states</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Monthly maintenance is not the ceiling.</strong> &ldquo;The father&rsquo;s responsibility is not exhausted by the fact that he has paid a fixed sum towards monthly maintenance.&rdquo; Where earlier orders cover education and medical needs, those accrue in addition.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The mother is a conduit, not the claimant.</strong> &ldquo;The payment may pass through the hands of the mother, but its purpose is the welfare of the child.&rdquo; An accusation that the mother seeks to &ldquo;extract money&rdquo; is no answer to the child&rsquo;s established expenses.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Her salary is not his discount.</strong> &ldquo;A woman does not forfeit her child&rsquo;s entitlement to support because she has worked hard to become financially independent.&rdquo; And more broadly: &ldquo;The end of a marriage cannot be treated as the end of a father&rsquo;s responsibility.&rdquo;</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Quantification is not punishment.</strong> &ldquo;The amount directed to be paid is not a penalty imposed upon the petitioner for the failure of his marriage&rdquo; — it is the settling of an account already owed under subsisting orders.</div>
</div>

<h2>The &ldquo;gold digger&rdquo; observations, in context</h2>
<p>The phrase did not come from the record of this case; the court addressed, in general terms, a litigation culture in which men describe women seeking maintenance as &ldquo;gold diggers&rdquo; or &ldquo;profiteers&rdquo;. The court recorded its disapproval of attributing ulterior financial motives &ldquo;without adequate basis&rdquo;, called the tendency unfortunate, and said men who resort to such language &ldquo;must be firmly reminded&rdquo; that a demand for maintenance is not greed. The balance was equally explicit: the observations condemn a vocabulary, not a gender, and every claim still falls to be tested on its merits — with both parents expected to act responsibly.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>For claimants: keep the child&rsquo;s expense file contemporaneous — fee receipts, transport invoices, medical bills — because item-wise records are what survive revision.</p>
  <p>For paying parents: contest specific items with proof, not with general denials or character attacks; credited payments show good faith and are fully accounted for.</p>
  <p>For both: a recorded compromise is enforceable machinery, and leaving it half-performed — as with the unexecuted flat settlement here — only lengthens the litigation.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">Delhi&rsquo;s Family Courts apply the same framework under Section 144 BNSS: the child&rsquo;s welfare is the lodestar, both parents&rsquo; means matter, and accrued educational and medical expenses under subsisting orders are recoverable as quantified arrears. The Madurai Bench&rsquo;s contribution is tonal as much as legal — a reminder that the vocabulary of maintenance litigation is itself subject to judicial standards.</div>
